      "text": "The maximum possible length of $\\overline{OB}$ will be obtained when $\\overline{OB}$ is parallel to $\\overline{OA}$. In this case, the angle between $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{OA}$ is $60^\\circ$ since the angle between the two rays is $30^\\circ$. We can use the Law of Cosines to obtain an equation for the length of $\\overline{OB}$. Let $x$ represent the length of $\\overline{OB}$. Then,\n\\begin{align*}\n1^2 &= x^2 + (x+2)^2 - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot (x+2) \\cos (60^\\circ) \\\\\n&= x^2 + x^2 + 4x + 4 - 2x(x+2) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^2 + 2x + 4 - x^2 - 2x\\\\\n&= x^2 + 4\n\\end{align*}\n\nSolving for x yields\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 + 4 &= 1 \\\\\nx^2 &= -3\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince the square root of a negative number is not a real number, there is no positive possible value for $x$ that satisfies the equation. Hence, there is no maximum possible length for $\\overline{OB}$.",
